# Docs: no machine-specific absolute paths

1. **Never write a machine-specific absolute path into documentation.** That means no
   `/Users/<name>/…`, `/home/<name>/…`, `/root/…`, `C:\…`, and no `~/`-rooted path outside the
   portable dotfile roots (`~/.claude`, `~/.config`, `~/.cursor`, `~/.local`, `~/.ssh`).
2. **Use repo-relative paths instead.** Reference files as `src/simulation/topologicalEval.ts`,
   and workspace siblings as `../web-ide/simulator/src/chip/chip.ts`. For shell snippets, say
   "from the repo root" rather than `cd`-ing to a hardcoded location.
3. **Enforced, not merely advised.** `scripts/check-doc-paths.mjs` runs in `.husky/pre-commit`
   (staged docs) and in CI (every tracked doc). Detection logic and its unit tests live in
   `scripts/hooks/docPaths.logic.mjs` / `docPaths.logic.test.mjs`.
4. **Escape hatch, used sparingly.** If a doc genuinely must quote a real absolute path, mark that
   line with `<!-- allow-abs-path -->` and it is skipped.
5. **Vendored trees are exempt** (`.claude/skills/` except `hacer-patterns/` and `docs-sync/`,
   `.cursor/`, `node_modules/`) because `scripts/sync-superpowers.sh` overwrites them upstream.
6. **Cited paths must exist** in `REPO_MAP.md` and `AGENTS.md`: the same script checks every
   backticked path and link target against the tree (`DEAD PATH file:line path`). Describe a
   planned file without a path, or mark the line `<!-- allow-missing-path -->`. See ADR-0014.

**Why:** an absolute path is true on exactly one machine. It is useless to any other contributor or
agent, and it rots the moment a directory is renamed — which is precisely what happened when the
workspace moved from `slow/` to `ha/`.
